2026-07-22T12:19:17.4039976Z     The ``pytest`` framework makes it easy to write small tests, yet
2026-07-22T12:19:17.4040665Z     scales to support complex functional testing for applications and libraries.
2026-07-22T12:19:17.4041058Z     
2026-07-22T12:19:17.4041275Z     An example of a simple test:
2026-07-22T12:19:17.4041535Z     
2026-07-22T12:19:17.4041747Z     .. code-block:: python
2026-07-22T12:19:17.4041985Z     
2026-07-22T12:19:17.4042192Z         # content of test_sample.py
2026-07-22T12:19:17.4042472Z         def inc(x):
2026-07-22T12:19:17.4042697Z             return x + 1
2026-07-22T12:19:17.4042921Z     
2026-07-22T12:19:17.4043108Z     
2026-07-22T12:19:17.4043312Z         def test_answer():
2026-07-22T12:19:17.4043561Z             assert inc(3) == 5
2026-07-22T12:19:17.4043799Z     
2026-07-22T12:19:17.4043988Z     
2026-07-22T12:19:17.4044185Z     To execute it::
2026-07-22T12:19:17.4044394Z     
2026-07-22T12:19:17.4044586Z         $ pytest
2026-07-22T12:19:17.4044883Z         ============================= test session starts =============================
2026-07-22T12:19:17.4045247Z         collected 1 items
2026-07-22T12:19:17.4045478Z     
2026-07-22T12:19:17.4045679Z         test_sample.py F
2026-07-22T12:19:17.4045920Z     
2026-07-22T12:19:17.4046307Z         ================================== FAILURES ===================================
2026-07-22T12:19:17.4046742Z         _________________________________ test_answer _________________________________
2026-07-22T12:19:17.4047098Z     
2026-07-22T12:19:17.4047303Z             def test_answer():
2026-07-22T12:19:17.4047572Z         >       assert inc(3) == 5
2026-07-22T12:19:17.4047845Z         E       assert 4 == 5
2026-07-22T12:19:17.4048108Z         E        +  where 4 = inc(3)
2026-07-22T12:19:17.4048372Z     
2026-07-22T12:19:17.4048588Z         test_sample.py:5: AssertionError
2026-07-22T12:19:17.4048948Z         ========================== 1 failed in 0.04 seconds ===========================
2026-07-22T12:19:17.4049285Z     
2026-07-22T12:19:17.4049473Z     
2026-07-22T12:19:17.4050315Z     Thanks to ``pytest``'s detailed assertion introspection, you can simply use plain ``assert`` statements. See `getting-started <https://docs.pytest.org/en/stable/getting-started.html#our-first-test-run>`_ for more examples.
